<!--
doc/src/sgml/ref/cypher_alter_vlabel.sgml
PostgreSQL documentation
-->

<refentry id="cypher-altervlabel">
 <indexterm zone="cypher-altervlabel">
  <primary>ALTER VLABEL</primary>
 </indexterm>

 <refmeta>
  <refentrytitle>ALTER VLABEL</refentrytitle>
  <manvolnum>7</manvolnum>
  <refmiscinfo>Cypher - Language Statements</refmiscinfo>
 </refmeta>

 <refnamediv>
  <refname>ALTER VLABEL</refname>
  <refpurpose>change a vertex label</refpurpose>
 </refnamediv>

 <refsynopsisdiv>
<synopsis>
ALTER VLABEL [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">action</replaceable> [, ...]
ALTER VLABEL [ IF EXISTS ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable> RENAME TO <replaceable class="parameter">new_label_name</replaceable>

<phrase>where <replaceable class="parameter">action</replaceable> is one of:</phrase>

    ADD [ COLUMN ] <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable>
    DROP [ COLUMN ] <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> [ RESTRICT | CASCADE ]
    DISABLE INDEX
    SET STORAGE { PLAIN | EXTERNAL | EXTENDED | MAIN }
    SET TABLESPACE <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable>
    SET { LOGGED | UNLOGGED }
    CLUSTER ON <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable>
    SET WITHOUT CLUSTER
    INHERIT <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable>
    NO INHERIT <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable>
    REPLICA IDENTITY { DEFAULT | USING INDEX <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable> | FULL | NOTHING }
    OWNER TO <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable>

<phrase>and <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable> is one of:</phrase>

    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ( <replaceable class="parameter">property_key</replaceable> )
    <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <replaceable class="parameter">data_type</replaceable> GENERATED ALWAYS AS ( <replaceable class="parameter">expression</replaceable> ) [ STORED ]
</synopsis>
 </refsynopsisdiv>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-altervlabel-description">
  <title>Description</title>

  <para>
   <command>ALTER VLABEL</command> renames a vertex label, changes its storage, attaches or
   detaches a parent, turns its indexes off, or adds and removes promoted typed columns. The
   label is looked up in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>, and the name cannot
   carry the graph. An edge label is refused; the statement for one is
   <xref linkend="cypher-alterelabel"/>, which accepts every action listed here.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>RENAME TO</literal> is its own form. It cannot be combined with any other action,
   and a list of several actions cannot contain it. Several actions in one statement are
   separated by commas and applied in order. The base label <literal>ag_vertex</literal> cannot
   be altered at all, and cannot be attached or detached as a parent.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>ADD COLUMN</literal> promotes a property to a real typed column beside the property
   map and backfills it from the rows already there, so the column is complete the moment the
   statement returns, and the statement fails on the first existing value that does not fit.
   The three forms and their restrictions are the ones <xref linkend="cypher-createvlabel"/>
   sets out. The property key comes from the generation expression rather than from the column
   name, and is recorded in <structname>ag_label_property</structname>, so a query keeps naming
   the property and the column name never appears in Cypher.
  </para>

  <para>
   Reshaping the label's table with <command>ALTER TABLE</command> instead is refused unless
   <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is on, and so are renaming one of its columns, moving it
   to another schema, and a SQL <command>MERGE</command> that writes it. Changing the type of
   <structfield>id</structfield>, <structfield>properties</structfield>,
   <structfield>start</structfield> or <structfield>end</structfield> stays refused even with
   the setting on. See <xref linkend="agensgraph-config"/>. The actions on this page never need
   it, because they update the graph catalog themselves.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-altervlabel-parameters">
  <title>Parameters</title>

  <variablelist>
   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>IF EXISTS</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Issue a notice instead of an error when the label does not exist.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><replaceable class="parameter">label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Label to alter, in the graph named by <varname>graph_path</varname>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>RENAME TO</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">new_label_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Give the label a new name. Vertices keep their ids, so <function>label()</function> reports
      the new name for rows created before the rename. The sequence and indexes keep theirs.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>ADD [ COLUMN ]</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">label_prop</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Add a promoted typed column and backfill it. A property already promoted on the label is
      refused, as is promoting the same key twice in one statement.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DROP [ COLUMN ]</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">column_name</replaceable> <literal>[ RESTRICT | CASCADE ]</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Drop a column and, for a promoted property, forget its mapping, so reads of that key go
      back to the property map, which still holds the value. Indexes on the column go with it,
      and a structural column of the label is refused. <literal>RESTRICT</literal>, the default,
      refuses while another object depends on the column; <literal>CASCADE</literal> drops those.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Mark every index on the label invalid, so that a bulk load does not maintain them. Reads
      and writes keep working and the planner ignores an invalid index. There is no
      <literal>ENABLE INDEX</literal>; the way back is <xref linkend="cypher-reindex"/>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET STORAGE { PLAIN | EXTERNAL | EXTENDED | MAIN }</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Set the storage mode of the label's <structfield>properties</structfield> column, which
      decides whether a large property map is held inline or in the table's TOAST relation, and
      whether it is compressed. A label starts with <literal>EXTENDED</literal>, out of line and
      compressed. The mode applies to future writes and rewrites nothing already stored.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET TABLESPACE</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">tablespace_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Move the label's table to another tablespace. Its indexes stay where they are.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>SET { LOGGED | UNLOGGED }</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Turn write ahead logging for the label's table on or off. Its id sequence follows.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>CLUSTER ON</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Record an index for a later <command>CLUSTER</command> to use; it reorders nothing now.
      <literal>SET WITHOUT CLUSTER</literal> forgets that record.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>INHERIT</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">parent_label</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Add the label as a child of another vertex label in the same graph, so a pattern on the
      parent starts matching this label's rows. <literal>NO INHERIT</literal> removes it again.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>REPLICA IDENTITY { DEFAULT | USING INDEX <replaceable class="parameter">index_name</replaceable> | FULL | NOTHING }</literal></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Choose which old values a write logs to identify a row, for logical replication.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>

   <varlistentry>
    <term><literal>OWNER TO</literal> <replaceable class="parameter">role_specification</replaceable></term>
    <listitem>
     <para>
      Give the label to another role: a role name, <literal>CURRENT_ROLE</literal>,
      <literal>CURRENT_USER</literal> or <literal>SESSION_USER</literal>.
     </para>
    </listitem>
   </varlistentry>
  </variablelist>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-altervlabel-examples">
  <title>Examples</title>

  <para>
   These examples do not use the graph in <xref linkend="agensgraph-example-data"/>, but build a
   graph of their own and drop it at the end. A rename takes the label's rows with it:
<programlisting>
CREATE GRAPH altervdemo;
SET graph_path = altervdemo;
CREATE VLABEL film;
CREATE (:film {title: 'Arrival', year: 2016}), (:film {title: 'Dune', year: 2021});
ALTER VLABEL film RENAME TO movie;
MATCH (m:movie) RETURN count(m) AS movies;

 movies
--------
 2
(1 row)
</programlisting>
   Promote a property on a label that already holds rows. The existing rows are backfilled, so
   the column is complete when the statement returns:
<programlisting>
ALTER VLABEL movie ADD COLUMN year int GENERATED;
SELECT year FROM altervdemo.movie ORDER BY year;

 year
------
 2016
 2021
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
   <structname>ag_label_property</structname> maps a property key to the column that answers for
   it. The two names differ whenever the explicit key form was used, and Cypher reads the key:
<programlisting>
ALTER VLABEL movie ADD COLUMN released text GENERATED (releaseDate);
SELECT p.propname, a.attname FROM pg_catalog.ag_label_property p
  JOIN pg_catalog.ag_label l ON l.oid = p.laboid
  JOIN pg_attribute a ON a.attrelid = l.relid AND a.attnum = p.attnum
 WHERE l.relid = 'altervdemo.movie'::regclass ORDER BY p.attnum;

  propname   | attname
-------------+----------
 year        | year
 releasedate | released
(2 rows)
</programlisting>
   <literal>ALTER VLABEL movie DROP COLUMN released</literal> takes the mapping with the column,
   and reads of <literal>releasedate</literal> go back to the property map, which still holds
   the value. A structural column cannot be dropped at all, and the table cannot be reshaped
   with SQL while <varname>enable_graph_ddl</varname> is off:
<programlisting>
ALTER VLABEL movie DROP COLUMN properties;

ERROR:  cannot drop column "properties" of graph label "movie"
HINT:  It is a structural column of the graph label.
</programlisting>
<programlisting>
ALTER TABLE altervdemo.movie ADD COLUMN rank int;

ERROR:  cannot add a column to graph label "movie" with ALTER TABLE
HINT:  Use ALTER VLABEL or ALTER ELABEL instead, which keeps the graph catalog in step.
</programlisting>
   <literal>DROP GRAPH altervdemo CASCADE</literal> drops the graph the examples built.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-altervlabel-notes">
  <title>Notes</title>

  <para>
   A rename does not rename the label's sequence or its indexes. After
   <literal>ALTER VLABEL film RENAME TO movie</literal> the primary key is still
   <literal>film_pkey</literal>, so <literal>ALTER VLABEL movie CLUSTER ON movie_pkey</literal>
   fails with <literal>index "movie_pkey" for table "movie" does not exist</literal>.
  </para>

  <para>
   <literal>SET STORAGE</literal> takes four keywords, not five. <literal>DEFAULT</literal> is
   reserved and is a syntax error here; write <literal>SET STORAGE extended</literal> to go back
   to the mode a label starts with. The <literal>IF EXISTS</literal> notice differs between the
   two forms: <literal>label "nosuch" does not exist, skipping</literal> from
   <literal>RENAME TO</literal>, and <literal>graph label "nosuch" does not exist,
   skipping</literal> from an action list.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

 <refsect1 id="cypher-altervlabel-compatibility">
  <title>Compatibility</title>

  <para>
   ISO/IEC 39075:2024 has no statement that alters a label, because it has no labels. An element
   type belongs to a graph type, created and dropped whole in clause 12.6, and nothing there
   corresponds to renaming a label, promoting a property, or turning an index off.
  </para>

  <para>
   Every action here is an <command>ALTER TABLE</command> action and means what it means there,
   with three differences. <literal>SET STORAGE</literal> names no column and applies the mode
   to <structfield>properties</structfield>. <literal>DISABLE INDEX</literal> has no counterpart
   at all: it marks the label's indexes invalid, which SQL can otherwise only do by interrupting
   <command>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</command>. And <literal>ADD COLUMN</literal> takes only the
   promoted forms, where <command>ALTER TABLE</command> takes any column definition.
  </para>
 </refsect1>

